.TH AGENT-SANDBOX-EXEC-DENYLIST 5 "2026-07-01" "agent-sandbox-exec 0.1.3" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
agent-sandbox-exec-denylist \- secret file denylists for agent-sandbox-exec
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B agent-sandbox-exec
enforces two denylists per sandboxed uid, applied as a union to that uid's
cgroup only:
.TP
.I /etc/agent-sandbox-exec/denylist
Root-controlled
.I base
list, applied to every sandboxed uid. A dpkg conffile \(em edits survive
upgrades. Use it for cluster-wide mandatory denies.
.TP
.I ~/.config/agent-sandbox-exec/denylist
Per-user
.I home
list, read from the uid's home directory (NFS-mounted is fine). Because deny
entries are scoped to the user's own cgroup, a user-controlled list can only
block opens inside that user's own sandbox.
.PP
Opens of listed files by sandboxed processes (and every process they spawn) are
denied by the BPF-LSM program with
.BR EPERM .
.SH "LOADING AND RELOAD"
A uid's denylist (base \(cu home) is re-read from disk on
.I every
launch request and diff-applied to that uid's cgroup, so an edit takes effect
on the user's
.I next
launch \(em no daemon restart needed and no effect on other uids. This is a
ping-driven reload (the launcher's migration request also triggers the re-read):
there is no inotify watch on the denylists and no polling, so an NFS-mounted
home is fine (no filesystem events are assumed). The apply is an incremental
diff (new entries inserted first, then stale ones removed), so the live deny set
is never emptied below the desired set.
.PP
A daemon restart
.RB ( "systemctl restart agent-sandbox-execd" )
is only needed to refresh the deny set of a uid that has a
.I running
sandbox but issues no new launch: on restart the daemon re-scans every existing
per-uid cgroup, restores the membership gate, and re-reads each uid's list from
disk.
.SH FORMAT
One path per line, in either denylist:
.PP
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
an absolute path to a
.I regular file
(no directories, no wildcards);
.IP \(bu 2
blank lines and lines beginning with
.B #
are ignored;
.IP \(bu 2
the file must exist
.RI ( agent-sandbox-execd
stats each path to resolve its inode; a missing or non-regular path is skipped
with a warning);
.IP \(bu 2
in the
.I home
list, symlink entries are rejected (skipped with a warning) for
predictability \(em the home list is user-controlled and a symlink that later
retargets would be a self-surprise. The base list follows symlinks normally
(root-controlled).
.RE
.PP
Matching is by
.I inode
.RI ( {cgroup-id, device, inode} ),
so hardlinks and bind-mounts of a listed file are also blocked. Each path is
re-`stat`'d on every launch, so an atomic replace of a secret (a new inode) is
picked up on the user's next launch.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.nf
# /etc/agent-sandbox-exec/denylist \(em cluster-wide base list
/home/owner/.ssh/id_rsa
/home/owner/.aws/credentials

# ~/.config/agent-sandbox-exec/denylist \(em per-user additions
/home/owner/.config/other-tool/token
.fi
.SH CAVEATS
.PP
Only the content read is blocked;
.BR stat (2)
(metadata) of a listed file still succeeds. A process
.I outside
the sandbox opening a secret and passing the fd via a Unix socket can bypass
this scheme. Denylist changes take effect on the user's next launch (no
restart needed); a restart only refreshes a running sandbox that has no new
launch.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR agent-sandbox-exec (1)